{"id":"gen-1790978595-dk7kzmtWMizeqoxImmmm","object":"chat.completion","created":1790978595,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6432-c2-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c2-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6432-c2-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c2\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            3,\n            1,\n            1\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6432\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c1-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-proposer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve the CSP described. The task: \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c2, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nSo we need to find all possible assignments for variables e, f, g, h that satisfy all constraints that involve those variables. The CSP includes constraints c1 through c20. The component c2 variables are e,f,g,h. Let's list constraints that involve these variables:\n\nc1: variables a,b\nc2: variables b,c\nc3: variables c,d\nc4: variables d,a\nc5: variables e,f\nc6: variables f,g\nc7: variables g,h\nc8: variables h,e\nc9: variables i,j\nc10: variables j,k\nc11: variables k,l\nc12: variables l,i\nc13: variables m,n\nc14: variables n,o\nc15: variables o,p\nc16: variables p,m\nc17: variables b,e\nc18: variables f,i\nc19: variables j,m\nc20: variables n,a\n\nThus constraints directly involving c2 variables are:\n- c5: (e,f)\n- c6: (f,g)\n- c7: (g,h)\n- c8: (h,e)\n- c17: (b,e) (b is from c1)\n- c18: (f,i) (i from c3)\n- c19: (j,m) (j from c3, m from c4)\n- c20: (n,a) (n from c4, a from c1)\n\nSo the c2 variables appear in constraints with other components. So we need to find all assignments for e,f,g,h that can be part of a global satisfying assignment for all variables a-p.\n\nThe problem asks to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c2 (i.e., all possible values of (e,f,g,h) that can appear in some full assignment satisfying all constraints). So we need to consider the entire CSP, find all possible tuples for (e,f,g,h) that are consistent with constraints.\n\nWe need to output a JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing all such tuples (including maybe multiple rows). The envelope template is given, with placeholder \"tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ]. We need to replace that with the full list.\n\nWe need to produce exactly one JSON envelope matching the template, no extra text, no markdown. So we need to compute the tuples.\n\nApproach: Solve the CSP fully. There are 16 variables each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints are binary, each specifying allowed pairs for a given ordered pair of variables.\n\nWe need to find all solutions. Then collect all distinct values of (e,f,g,h) across all solutions. Then sort lexicographically.\n\nWe need to do this manually? It's a fairly small CSP but many constraints. We could try to solve systematically.\n\nFirst, let's list constraints for each pair:\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed pairs: [3,3], [3,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,0]. So allowed (a,b):\n(3,3)\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,2)\n(3,0)\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed: [3,2], [0,0], [0,2], [1,0], [2,2], [1,3]\nSo (b,c):\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed: [2,3], [0,2], [2,2], [0,1], [1,3], [1,1]\nSo (c,d):\n(2,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed: [3,3], [0,2], [3,1], [3,0], [1,1], [3,2]\nSo (d,a):\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed: [1,3], [2,1], [0,3], [1,0], [2,2], [0,1]\nSo (e,f):\n(1,3)\n(2,1)\n(0,3)\n(1,0)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed: [3,1], [2,1], [0,1], [2,0], [0,3], [2,3]\nSo (f,g):\n(3,1)\n(2,1)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n(2,3)\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed: [1,1], [1,0], [0,2], [3,1], [0,1], [3,3]\nSo (g,h):\n(1,1)\n(1,0)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(0,1)\n(3,3)\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed: [1,1], [3,1], [0,3], [2,2], [3,2], [2,0]\nSo (h,e):\n(1,1)\n(3,1)\n(0,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,2)\n(2,0)\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed: [0,2], [0,3], [2,3], [1,1], [3,2], [1,0]\nSo (i,j):\n(0,2)\n(0,3)\n(2,3)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(1,0)\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed: [2,2], [1,3], [1,0], [1,1], [0,0], [0,3]\nSo (j,k):\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,0)\n(1,1)\n(0,0)\n(0,3)\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed: [2,0], [1,1], [3,3], [1,2], [0,1], [3,2]\nSo (k,l):\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,3)\n(1,2)\n(0,1)\n(3,2)\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed: [0,0], [2,0], [2,2], [3,1], [3,2], [2,3]\nSo (l,i):\n(0,0)\n(2,0)\n(2,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n\nc13 (m,n): allowed: [0,3], [2,2], [0,2], [1,3], [2,3], [3,1]\nSo (m,n):\n(0,3)\n(2,2)\n(0,2)\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(3,1)\n\nc14 (n,o): allowed: [3,2], [2,3], [0,0], [1,3], [2,1], [1,1]\nSo (n,o):\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n(0,0)\n(1,3)\n(2,1)\n(1,1)\n\nc15 (o,p): allowed: [2,0], [3,1], [2,2], [1,2], [1,0], [3,3]\nSo (o,p):\n(2,0)\n(3,1)\n(2,2)\n(1,2)\n(1,0)\n(3,3)\n\nc16 (p,m): allowed: [0,0], [1,1], [3,2], [2,3], [1,0], [3,0]\nSo (p,m):\n(0,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n(1,0)\n(3,0)\n\nc17 (b,e): allowed: [3,1], [1,1]\nSo (b,e):\n(3,1)\n(1,1)\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed: [3,0], [1,3]\nSo (f,i):\n(3,0)\n(1,3)\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed: [2,0], [3,1]\nSo (j,m):\n(2,0)\n(3,1)\n\nc20 (n,a): allowed: [3,3], [3,0]\nSo (n,a):\n(3,3)\n(3,0)\n\nThus we have all constraints.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible tuples for (e,f,g,h). So we need to solve the CSP.\n\nWe can treat it as a constraint satisfaction problem with 16 variables. Since domain size is 4, naive enumeration would be 4^16 ~ 4.3e9, too many. But constraints are limited. We can try to reduce using constraint propagation.\n\nWe can try to solve by constructing possible values for each variable given constraints.\n\nWe have a cycle of constraints linking variables across components:\n\nc1: a,b\nc2: b,c\nc3: c,d\nc4: d,a\n\nThus a,b,c,d form a 4-cycle.\n\nc5-c8: e,f,g,h form a 4-cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e.\n\nc9-c12: i,j,k,l form a 4-cycle: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i.\n\nc13-c16: m,n,o,p form a 4-cycle: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m.\n\nAdditionally cross constraints linking these cycles:\n\nc17: b-e\nc18: f-i\nc19: j-m\nc20: n-a\n\nThus we have a larger graph.\n\nGoal: find all solutions.\n\nWe can try to solve using reasoning.\n\nFirst, constraints c1-c4 linking a,b,c,d.\n\nLet's list all possible assignments for (a,b,c,d) satisfying c1-c4.\n\nc1: allowed (a,b):\n(3,3)\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,2)\n(3,0)\n\nc2: allowed (b,c):\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n\nc3: allowed (c,d):\n(2,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\nc4: allowed (d,a):\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n\nWe need to find all combos (a,b,c,d) consistent with these.\n\nWe could enumerate possible values for a and b from c1, then possible c from c2 given b, then d from c3 given c, then check c4 for d-a.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nFirst, list possible (a,b) from c1:\n\n1. (a=3, b=3)\n2. (3,2)\n3. (3,1)\n4. (1,3)\n5. (1,2)\n6. (3,0)\n\nNow for each, find possible c from c2 given b.\n\nc2 allowed pairs (b,c):\n- If b=3: allowed c values? The pairs: (3,2) only? Actually c2 includes (3,2) and (1,3). So if b=3, c can be 2 (from (3,2)) or 3 (from (1,3)). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- If b=2: pairs: (3,2) (b=3 not 2). Actually (3,2) has b=3. So not. (0,0) b=0. (0,2) b=0. (1,0) b=1. (2,2) b=2, c=2. (1,3) b=1. So only (2,2) yields b=2. So if b=2, c=2.\n\n- If b=1: pairs: (1,0) (c=0), (1,3) (c=3). So c ∈ {0,3}.\n\n- If b=0: pairs: (0,0) c=0, (0,2) c=2. So c ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow for each (a,b) from c1, we need to see if b is among {3,2,1,0}. All are.\n\nNow for each (a,b) and possible c, we need to find d from c3 (c,d). c3 allowed pairs (c,d):\n(2,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\nThus for a given c, possible d values are:\n\n- If c=2: d can be 3 (from (2,3)), 2 (from (2,2)). So d ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- If c=3: d can be 1 (from (1,3)). Actually c=3 appears only in (1,3) where c=1? Wait (1,3) is (c=1,d=3). So c=3 does not appear. So c=3 yields no d? Check c3 list: c values present: 2,0,2,0,1,1. So c values allowed: 0,1,2. No 3. So c cannot be 3. So any (b,c) where c=3 is invalid. So we must discard any combos where c=3.\n\nThus c must be 0,1,2.\n\nNow for each c, possible d:\n\n- c=0: d ∈ {2 (from (0,2)), 1 (from (0,1))}. So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- c=1: d ∈ {3 (from (1,3)), 1 (from (1,1))}. So d ∈ {3,1}.\n\n- c=2: d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nNow we need to check c4: allowed (d,a):\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n\nThus for each (d,a) pair, we need to ensure that the pair is in this list.\n\nNow we can enumerate all combos.\n\nLet's enumerate each (a,b) from c1, then possible c from c2, then d from c3, then check c4.\n\nWe'll do systematic enumeration.\n\n### Case 1: (a=3, b=3)\n\n- b=3 => c ∈ {2,3} from c2.\nBut c cannot be 3 (invalid). So c must be 2.\n\nThus c=2.\n\nNow c=2 => d ∈ {3,2} from c3.\n\nCheck each d:\n\n- d=3: Check c4 (d=3, a=3). Is (3,3) allowed? Yes, (3,3) is in c4 allowed pairs.\n\n- d=2: Check (d=2, a=3). Is (2,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs: (3,3), (0,2), (3,1), (3,0), (1,1), (3,2). (2,3) is not present. So (2,3) not allowed. So d=2 invalid.\n\nThus only (a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\nSo one solution for (a,b,c,d): (3,3,2,3).\n\n### Case 2: (a=3, b=2)\n\n- b=2 => c must be 2 (from c2). So c=2.\n\nc=2 => d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nCheck d=3: (d=3,a=3) allowed? (3,3) yes.\n\nd=2: (2,3) not allowed. So only d=3 works.\n\nThus (a=3,b=2,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\n### Case 3: (a=3, b=1)\n\n- b=1 => c ∈ {0,3} from c2. c cannot be 3, so c=0.\n\nc=0 => d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCheck d=2: (d=2,a=3) allowed? (2,3) not allowed. So invalid.\n\nd=1: (1,3) allowed? c4 includes (3,1) but that's (d=3,a=1). We need (d=1,a=3). Check c4 list: (3,3), (0,2), (3,1), (3,0), (1,1), (3,2). (1,3) not present. So invalid.\n\nThus no solution for (a=3,b=1).\n\n### Case 4: (a=1, b=3)\n\n- b=3 => c ∈ {2,3}, c cannot be 3, so c=2.\n\nc=2 => d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nCheck d=3: (d=3,a=1) allowed? c4 includes (3,1) which is (d=3,a=1). Yes.\n\nd=2: (2,1) allowed? c4 includes (3,2) but that's (d=3,a=2). (2,1) not present. So only d=3 works.\n\nThus (a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\n### Case 5: (a=1, b=2)\n\n- b=2 => c=2.\n\nc=2 => d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nCheck d=3: (3,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nd=2: (2,1) not allowed. So only d=3.\n\nThus (a=1,b=2,c=2,d=3) valid.\n\n### Case 6: (a=3, b=0)\n\n- b=0 => c ∈ {0,2}.\n\nConsider each:\n\nc=0: then d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- d=2: (d=2,a=3) not allowed.\n\n- d=1: (1,3) not allowed.\n\nThus c=0 yields no solution.\n\nc=2: then d ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- d=3: (3,3) allowed? Yes (a=3). So (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3) works.\n\n- d=2: (2,3) not allowed.\n\nThus (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\nThus we have found all (a,b,c,d) solutions:\n\nList them:\n\n1. (a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3)\n2. (a=3,b=2,c=2,d=3)\n3. (a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3)\n4. (a=1,b=2,c=2,d=3)\n5. (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3)\n\nCheck if any other combos? Let's double-check each case for completeness.\n\nWe enumerated all six (a,b) from c1. Cases 1-6 gave solutions for cases 1,2,4,5,6. Cases 3 and maybe others? Let's double-check case 3 (a=3,b=1) gave none. Case 5 (a=1,b=2) gave solution. So total 5 solutions for (a,b,c,d). All have d=3. Interesting.\n\nThus (a,b,c,d) are constrained heavily: a can be 3 or 1, b can be 3,2,0, c always 2, d always 3. Let's verify each solution:\n\n- (3,3,2,3)\n- (3,2,2,3)\n- (1,3,2,3)\n- (1,2,2,3)\n- (3,0,2,3)\n\nThus a ∈ {1,3}, b ∈ {0,2,3} (but not 1), c=2, d=3.\n\nNow we also have cross constraints linking these to other variables:\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (3,1) or (1,1). So (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,1). So e is determined by b:\n\n- If b=3 => e must be 1 (since (3,1) is allowed). (1,1) would require b=1, not applicable.\n\n- If b=1 => e must be 1 (since (1,1) allowed). But b cannot be 1 in our solutions. So b=1 not possible.\n\n- If b=0 => no allowed pair, because allowed pairs for (b,e) are only (3,1) and (1,1). So b cannot be 0. But we have a solution with b=0 (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3). That would violate c17. So that solution is invalid overall. So we must discard any solution where b=0. So (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3) is invalid due to c17.\n\nThus remaining (a,b,c,d) solutions are those where b ∈ {2,3}. Let's check each:\n\n- (3,3,2,3): b=3 OK.\n- (3,2,2,3): b=2? But c17 only allows b=3 or b=1. b=2 is not allowed. So (3,2,2,3) invalid.\n- (1,3,2,3): b=3 OK.\n- (1,2,2,3): b=2 invalid.\n\nThus only solutions where b=3 survive: (a,b,c,d) = (3,3,2,3) and (1,3,2,3). So two possible assignments for (a,b,c,d) that satisfy c1-c4 and c17.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for (a,b,c,d):\nS1: a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3\nS2: a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3\n\nNow we need to consider constraints linking to other components: c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m), c20 (n,a). Also c5-c8 linking e,f,g,h and c9-c12 linking i,j,k,l and c13-c16 linking m,n,o,p.\n\nAlso there are constraints linking these cycles via cross constraints: c17 (b,e) already used to restrict e based on b; c18 (f,i); c19 (j,m); c20 (n,a).\n\nThus we need to find all assignments for the rest of variables consistent with these constraints and the cycles.\n\nWe also have constraints linking within each cycle: c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) for e,f,g,h. So they form a 4-cycle.\n\nSimilarly i,j,k,l cycle: c9 (i,j), c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i).\n\nSimilarly m,n,o,p cycle: c13 (m,n), c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m).\n\nThus we have three independent cycles plus cross constraints linking b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a.\n\nGiven we have two possible (a,b) values (a=3,b=3) or (a=1,b=3). In both cases b=3. So b is fixed to 3. So we have b=3 for both solutions.\n\nThus c17 (b,e) => (3,e) must be allowed. Allowed pairs: (3,1) only (since (1,1) not applicable). So e must be 1. So e=1 for both solutions.\n\nThus e is fixed: e=1.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (1,3), (2,1), (0,3), (1,0), (2,2), (0,1). Since e=1, we need (1,f) in allowed list. Let's check allowed pairs for (e,f) where e=1:\n\nFrom c5 allowed pairs:\n- (1,3) => f=3\n- (1,0) => f=0\n- (1,?) any others? (2,1) e=2, not. (0,3) e=0. (2,2) e=2. (0,1) e=0.\n\nThus only (1,3) and (1,0) are possible for e=1. So f ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus f can be 3 or 0.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (3,1), (2,1), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3), (2,3). So depending on f.\n\n- If f=3: allowed g values from c6: (3,1) => g=1. Also (3,?) any others? No other pair with f=3. So g=1.\n\n- If f=0: allowed g values: (0,1) => g=1; (0,3) => g=3; (0,?) also (0,?) maybe (0,?) only those two. So g ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus possible combos:\n\nCase A: f=3 => g=1.\n\nCase B: f=0 => g ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (1,1), (1,0), (0,2), (3,1), (0,1), (3,3). So given g, possible h values:\n\n- If g=1: allowed h: (1,1) => h=1; (1,0) => h=0. Also (3,1) is g=3 not relevant. So h ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h: (3,1) => h=1; (3,3) => h=3. So h ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus for each case:\n\nCase A: f=3,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0}.\n\nCase B1: f=0,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0}.\n\nCase B2: f=0,g=3 => h ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (1,1), (3,1), (0,3), (2,2), (3,2), (2,0). Since e=1, we need (h,1) allowed. So we need pairs where second component is 1: (1,1) => h=1; (3,1) => h=3. Also (0,3) second is 3 not relevant; (2,2) second is 2; (3,2) second 2; (2,0) second 0. So only allowed h values with e=1 are h=1 (via (1,1)) and h=3 (via (3,1)). So h must be either 1 or 3.\n\nThus from earlier possibilities, we need to intersect with allowed h values {1,3}.\n\nLet's examine each case:\n\nCase A: f=3,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0} from c7. Intersection with {1,3} yields h=1 only. So h=1.\n\nThus case A yields (f=3,g=1,h=1). Check c8: (h=1,e=1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is allowed.\n\nCase B1: f=0,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0}. Intersection with {1,3} yields h=1 only. So h=1.\n\nThus (f=0,g=1,h=1). Check c8: (1,1) allowed.\n\nCase B2: f=0,g=3 => h ∈ {1,3}. Intersection with {1,3} yields h ∈ {1,3}. So both h=1 and h=3 possible.\n\nThus we have three possible subcases:\n\n- Subcase 1: f=3,g=1,h=1.\n- Subcase 2: f=0,g=1,h=1.\n- Subcase 3a: f=0,g=3,h=1.\n- Subcase 3b: f=0,g=3,h=3.\n\nNow we also have cross constraint c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (3,0) and (1,3). So (f,i) must be either (3,0) or (1,3). So we need to consider f values and possible i.\n\nGiven f possibilities: f=3 or f=0.\n\n- If f=3: then (f,i) must be (3,0). So i=0.\n\n- If f=0: then (f,i) must be (0,?) but allowed pairs are (3,0) and (1,3). No (0,?) pair. So f=0 is not allowed because there is no allowed pair with f=0. Wait check c18: allowed pairs: [3,0], [1,3]. So (f,i) can be (3,0) or (1,3). So f can be 3 (with i=0) or f can be 1 (with i=3). But we have f ∈ {3,0} from earlier. So f=0 is not allowed because there is no allowed pair with f=0. So f cannot be 0. So the only viable f is 3. So f must be 3, i=0.\n\nThus we discard all subcases where f=0. So only Subcase 1 remains: f=3,g=1,h=1,i=0.\n\nThus e=1, f=3, g=1, h=1, i=0.\n\nNow we have determined e,f,g,h uniquely: (e=1,f=3,g=1,h=1). So the tuple for c2 is (e,f,g,h) = (1,3,1,1). That's one tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify all constraints are satisfied. Let's verify all constraints involving these variables:\n\nc5 (e,f): (1,3) is allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c5 allowed pairs.\n\nc6 (f,g): (3,1) is allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c6.\n\nc7 (g,h): (1,1) is allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c7.\n\nc8 (h,e): (1,1) is allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c8.\n\nc17 (b,e): b=3, e=1 => (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is allowed.\n\nc18 (f,i): f=3,i=0 => (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus all constraints for c2 and cross constraints are satisfied.\n\nNow we need to consider the rest of the variables: i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p and constraints c9-c16, plus cross constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). Also constraints linking these cycles.\n\nWe have determined i=0. Now we need to find all possible assignments for the rest that satisfy constraints.\n\nNow we need to consider the cycle i-j-k-l (c9-c12). Variables: i=0, j, k, l.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (2,3), (1,1), (3,2), (1,0). Since i=0, we need (0,j) allowed. Allowed pairs with i=0: (0,2) => j=2; (0,3) => j=3. Also (0,?) any others? (0,?) appears only in (0,2) and (0,3). So j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,3), (1,0), (1,1), (0,0), (0,3). So depending on j.\n\n- If j=2: allowed k: (2,2) => k=2. Also any other pairs with j=2? No other. So k=2.\n\n- If j=3: allowed k: (0,3) => k=3? Wait (0,3) is (j=0,k=3). Not j=3. (1,3) is (j=1,k=3). Not j=3. So there is no pair with j=3? Let's check c10 allowed pairs: [2,2], [1,3], [1,0], [1,1], [0,0], [0,3]. So j values present: 2,1,1,1,0,0. So j=3 does not appear. So j cannot be 3. Thus j must be 2.\n\nThus j=2, k=2.\n\nNow c11 (k,l): allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (1,2), (0,1), (3,2). Since k=2, we need (2,l) allowed. Check pairs with first component 2: (2,0) => l=0. Also (2,?) any others? No other. So l=0.\n\nThus l=0.\n\nNow c12 (l,i): allowed pairs: (0,0), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1), (3,2), (2,3). Since l=0, i=0, we need (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in c12 allowed pairs. So (l=0,i=0) is allowed.\n\nThus the i-j-k-l cycle is uniquely determined: (i=0,j=2,k=2,l=0).\n\nNow we have cross constraints: c19 (j,m): allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). Since j=2, we need (2,m) allowed. Allowed pairs: (2,0) => m=0. Also (3,1) not applicable. So m=0.\n\nThus m=0.\n\nNow we have c13 (m,n): allowed pairs: (0,3), (2,2), (0,2), (1,3), (2,3), (3,1). Since m=0, we need (0,n) allowed. Allowed pairs with first component 0: (0,3) => n=3; (0,2) => n=2. So n ∈ {3,2}.\n\nThus n ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c14 (n,o): allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (0,0), (1,3), (2,1), (1,1). So depending on n.\n\n- If n=2: allowed (2,3) => o=3; (2,1) => o=1. Also (0,0) not; (1,3) not; (3,2) not; (1,1) not. So o ∈ {3,1}.\n\n- If n=3: allowed (3,2) => o=2; (1,3) => o=3; (2,3) => o=3? Actually (2,3) is (n=2) not relevant. (1,1) not. So for n=3: (3,2) => o=2; (1,3) => o=3. Also maybe (2,3) is not n=3. So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus possible combos:\n\nn=2 => o ∈ {3,1}\nn=3 => o ∈ {2,3}\n\nNow c15 (o,p): allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1), (2,2), (1,2), (1,0), (3,3). So depending on o.\n\n- If o=3: allowed p: (3,1) => p=1; (3,3) => p=3. Also (1,2) not; (2,2) not; (2,0) not; (1,0) not.\n\nThus o=3 => p ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- If o=2: allowed p: (2,0) => p=0; (2,2) => p=2; (1,2) => p=2? Actually (1,2) is (o=2? No (1,2) is (o=2? Wait c15 is (o,p). So (1,2) means o=1,p=2. So not relevant. So for o=2: p ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- If o=1: allowed p: (1,2) => p=2; (1,0) => p=0. So p ∈ {2,0}.\n\nThus we need to consider each possible n and o, then p.\n\nNow c16 (p,m): allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3), (1,0), (3,0). Since m=0, we need (p,0) allowed. So allowed pairs where second component is 0: (0,0) => p=0; (1,0) => p=1; (3,0) => p=3. Also (2,3) second is 3; (3,2) second 2; (1,1) second 1. So p ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nThus p must be in {0,1,3}.\n\nNow we need to find assignments for n,o,p that satisfy c14, c15, c16 constraints, plus p ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nLet's enumerate possibilities.\n\nWe have two possible n values: 2 or 3.\n\n### n=2:\n\n- o ∈ {3,1}.\n\n#### n=2, o=3:\n\n- c15: o=3 => p ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {1,3} = {1,3}. So p ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus possible (n=2,o=3,p=1) or (n=2,o=3,p=3).\n\nCheck c16 for p=1: (p=1,m=0) => (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is allowed. For p=3: (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is allowed.\n\nThus both are valid.\n\n#### n=2, o=1:\n\n- o=1 => p ∈ {2,0} from c15.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {2,0} = {0}. So p must be 0.\n\nThus (n=2,o=1,p=0) is valid. Check c15: (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is allowed. c16: (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) allowed.\n\nThus (n=2,o=1,p=0) valid.\n\nThus for n=2 we have three possible assignments:\n\n1. (n=2, o=3, p=1)\n2. (n=2, o=3, p=3)\n3. (n=2, o=1, p=0)\n\n### n=3:\n\n- o ∈ {2,3}.\n\n#### n=3, o=2:\n\n- o=2 => p ∈ {0,2} from c15.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {0,2} = {0}. So p=0.\n\nThus (n=3, o=2, p=0) valid. Check c15: (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) allowed. c16: (0,0) allowed? Yes.\n\n#### n=3, o=3:\n\n- o=3 => p ∈ {1,3} from c15.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {1,3} = {1,3}. So p ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus (n=3, o=3, p=1) and (n=3, o=3, p=3) are valid.\n\nCheck c15: (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) allowed. (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) allowed. c16: (1,0) allowed? Yes. (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus for n=3 we have three possible assignments:\n\n1. (n=3, o=2, p=0)\n2. (n=3, o=3, p=1)\n3. (n=3, o=3, p=3)\n\nThus total possible assignments for (n,o,p) are 6.\n\nNow we also have cross constraint c20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (3,3) and (3,0). So (n,a) must be either (3,3) or (3,0). So n must be 3 (since both allowed pairs have n=3). So n must be 3. Therefore n=2 possibilities are invalid because n must be 3. So we discard all assignments where n=2.\n\nThus only assignments with n=3 remain.\n\nThus we have three possible assignments for (n,o,p) given n=3:\n\n- (n=3, o=2, p=0)\n- (n=3, o=3, p=1)\n- (n=3, o=3, p=3)\n\nNow we also need to consider any other constraints linking these variables. c13 (m,n) already satisfied with m=0,n=3? Let's check c13: allowed pairs (0,3) and (0,2) etc. (0,3) is allowed. So (m=0,n=3) is allowed.\n\nc14 (n,o) we already used.\n\nc15 (o,p) we used.\n\nc16 (p,m) we used.\n\nThus all constraints for m,n,o,p are satisfied for these three assignments.\n\nNow we also need to consider any constraints linking these to other components: cross constraints c19 (j,m) already satisfied (j=2,m=0). c20 (n,a) must be satisfied. Since a is either 3 or 1 (from earlier solutions). Let's check each possible a.\n\nWe have two possible (a,b,c,d) solutions: S1: a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3; S2: a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3.\n\nNow c20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (3,3) and (3,0). So n must be 3, a must be 3 (since (3,0) would require a=0). So a must be 3. So a=3 is required. Therefore the solution with a=1 is invalid. So we must discard S2 (a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3). So only S1 remains: a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3.\n\nThus we have a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3.\n\nNow we need to ensure all constraints are satisfied with this assignment.\n\nWe already checked c1-c4, c17, c5-c8, c9-c12, c13-c16, c18, c19, c20. Let's verify each for the final assignments.\n\nWe have determined:\n\n- a=3\n- b=3\n- c=2\n- d=3\n- e=1\n- f=3\n- g=1\n- h=1\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=2\n- l=0\n- m=0\n- n=3 (one of the three possibilities)\n- o and p depend on case.\n\nNow we need to verify all constraints for each of the three possible (n,o,p) assignments.\n\nConstraints list:\n\nc1 (a,b): (3,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc2 (b,c): (3,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nc3 (c,d): (2,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc4 (d,a): (3,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc5 (e,f): (1,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc6 (f,g): (3,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc7 (g,h): (1,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc8 (h,e): (1,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc9 (i,j): (0,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nc10 (j,k): (2,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nc11 (k,l): (2,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc12 (l,i): (0,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc13 (m,n): (0,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc14 (n,o): depends on o.\n\n- For (n=3,o=2): (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c14 allowed pairs.\n\n- For (n=3,o=3): (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is allowed.\n\nc15 (o,p): depends.\n\n- For (o=2,p=0): (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c15.\n\n- For (o=3,p=1): (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c15.\n\n- For (o=3,p=3): (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in c15.\n\nc16 (p,m): (p,0) must be allowed.\n\n- For p=0: (0,0) allowed? Yes.\n\n- For p=1: (1,0) allowed? Yes.\n\n- For p=3: (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc17 (b,e): (3,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc18 (f,i): (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc19 (j,m): (2,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc20 (n,a): (3,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied for all three possibilities of (n,o,p). So we have three distinct global solutions differing only in (n,o,p). However, the question asks to \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c2, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to collect all possible assignments for component c2 across all global solutions. Since c2 variables are e,f,g,h, which we have uniquely determined as (1,3,1,1) across all solutions (the three solutions share same e,f,g,h). So there is only one tuple for c2.\n\nThus the answer should be a JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing a single tuple: [1,3,1,1] (in order e,f,g,h). The envelope template currently has placeholder [[0,0,0,0]]. We need to replace that with the correct tuple(s). The envelope also includes other fields that we must keep unchanged except for the tuples. The envelope template includes fields like \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", etc. We need to output exactly that JSON with the updated tuples.\n\nThus final envelope:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6432-c2-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c2-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6432-c2-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c2\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            3,\n            1,\n            1\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6432\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c1-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure JSON formatting exactly matches. No extra spaces? The template has spaces. We can preserve similar formatting. The requirement: \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output raw JSON as above, with the tuple replaced. Ensure we keep the same ordering of keys as in template. The template includes \"payload\": { \"claim\": {...} }. The \"value\" includes \"component\": \"c2\", \"tuples\": [ [1,3,1,1] ], \"variables\": [ \"e\", \"f\", \"g\", \"h\" ].\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope with that tuple.\n\nWe should double-check that there are no other possible assignments for c2 that could arise from other global solutions that we might have missed. Let's verify if there could be any alternative assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints but we eliminated due to cross constraints. Our deduction that f must be 3 came from c18 requiring (f,i) be either (3,0) or (1,3). Since f ∈ {3,0} from c5-c8, only f=3 works. So f is forced to 3, i=0. Then g=1 forced, h=1 forced. So indeed e,f,g,h are uniquely determined. So only one tuple.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to output exactly the JSON envelope.\n\nOne thing: The envelope includes \"read_refs\" with two entries. The second entry references \"claim:c2-tuples\" with revision 0, state_revision 0. That's fine.\n\nThus final answer.\n\nWe must ensure that the JSON is valid. Use proper quoting. Ensure no trailing commas. Ensure \"tuples\" array contains exactly one tuple.\n\nThus final output.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve the CSP described. The task: \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c2, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nSo we need to find all possible assignments for variables e, f, g, h that satisfy all constraints that involve those variables. The CSP includes constraints c1 through c20. The component c2 variables are e,f,g,h. Let's list constraints that involve these variables:\n\nc1: variables a,b\nc2: variables b,c\nc3: variables c,d\nc4: variables d,a\nc5: variables e,f\nc6: variables f,g\nc7: variables g,h\nc8: variables h,e\nc9: variables i,j\nc10: variables j,k\nc11: variables k,l\nc12: variables l,i\nc13: variables m,n\nc14: variables n,o\nc15: variables o,p\nc16: variables p,m\nc17: variables b,e\nc18: variables f,i\nc19: variables j,m\nc20: variables n,a\n\nThus constraints directly involving c2 variables are:\n- c5: (e,f)\n- c6: (f,g)\n- c7: (g,h)\n- c8: (h,e)\n- c17: (b,e) (b is from c1)\n- c18: (f,i) (i from c3)\n- c19: (j,m) (j from c3, m from c4)\n- c20: (n,a) (n from c4, a from c1)\n\nSo the c2 variables appear in constraints with other components. So we need to find all assignments for e,f,g,h that can be part of a global satisfying assignment for all variables a-p.\n\nThe problem asks to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c2 (i.e., all possible values of (e,f,g,h) that can appear in some full assignment satisfying all constraints). So we need to consider the entire CSP, find all possible tuples for (e,f,g,h) that are consistent with constraints.\n\nWe need to output a JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing all such tuples (including maybe multiple rows). The envelope template is given, with placeholder \"tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ]. We need to replace that with the full list.\n\nWe need to produce exactly one JSON envelope matching the template, no extra text, no markdown. So we need to compute the tuples.\n\nApproach: Solve the CSP fully. There are 16 variables each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints are binary, each specifying allowed pairs for a given ordered pair of variables.\n\nWe need to find all solutions. Then collect all distinct values of (e,f,g,h) across all solutions. Then sort lexicographically.\n\nWe need to do this manually? It's a fairly small CSP but many constraints. We could try to solve systematically.\n\nFirst, let's list constraints for each pair:\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed pairs: [3,3], [3,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,0]. So allowed (a,b):\n(3,3)\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,2)\n(3,0)\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed: [3,2], [0,0], [0,2], [1,0], [2,2], [1,3]\nSo (b,c):\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed: [2,3], [0,2], [2,2], [0,1], [1,3], [1,1]\nSo (c,d):\n(2,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed: [3,3], [0,2], [3,1], [3,0], [1,1], [3,2]\nSo (d,a):\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed: [1,3], [2,1], [0,3], [1,0], [2,2], [0,1]\nSo (e,f):\n(1,3)\n(2,1)\n(0,3)\n(1,0)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed: [3,1], [2,1], [0,1], [2,0], [0,3], [2,3]\nSo (f,g):\n(3,1)\n(2,1)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n(2,3)\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed: [1,1], [1,0], [0,2], [3,1], [0,1], [3,3]\nSo (g,h):\n(1,1)\n(1,0)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(0,1)\n(3,3)\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed: [1,1], [3,1], [0,3], [2,2], [3,2], [2,0]\nSo (h,e):\n(1,1)\n(3,1)\n(0,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,2)\n(2,0)\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed: [0,2], [0,3], [2,3], [1,1], [3,2], [1,0]\nSo (i,j):\n(0,2)\n(0,3)\n(2,3)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(1,0)\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed: [2,2], [1,3], [1,0], [1,1], [0,0], [0,3]\nSo (j,k):\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,0)\n(1,1)\n(0,0)\n(0,3)\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed: [2,0], [1,1], [3,3], [1,2], [0,1], [3,2]\nSo (k,l):\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,3)\n(1,2)\n(0,1)\n(3,2)\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed: [0,0], [2,0], [2,2], [3,1], [3,2], [2,3]\nSo (l,i):\n(0,0)\n(2,0)\n(2,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n\nc13 (m,n): allowed: [0,3], [2,2], [0,2], [1,3], [2,3], [3,1]\nSo (m,n):\n(0,3)\n(2,2)\n(0,2)\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(3,1)\n\nc14 (n,o): allowed: [3,2], [2,3], [0,0], [1,3], [2,1], [1,1]\nSo (n,o):\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n(0,0)\n(1,3)\n(2,1)\n(1,1)\n\nc15 (o,p): allowed: [2,0], [3,1], [2,2], [1,2], [1,0], [3,3]\nSo (o,p):\n(2,0)\n(3,1)\n(2,2)\n(1,2)\n(1,0)\n(3,3)\n\nc16 (p,m): allowed: [0,0], [1,1], [3,2], [2,3], [1,0], [3,0]\nSo (p,m):\n(0,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n(1,0)\n(3,0)\n\nc17 (b,e): allowed: [3,1], [1,1]\nSo (b,e):\n(3,1)\n(1,1)\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed: [3,0], [1,3]\nSo (f,i):\n(3,0)\n(1,3)\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed: [2,0], [3,1]\nSo (j,m):\n(2,0)\n(3,1)\n\nc20 (n,a): allowed: [3,3], [3,0]\nSo (n,a):\n(3,3)\n(3,0)\n\nThus we have all constraints.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible tuples for (e,f,g,h). So we need to solve the CSP.\n\nWe can treat it as a constraint satisfaction problem with 16 variables. Since domain size is 4, naive enumeration would be 4^16 ~ 4.3e9, too many. But constraints are limited. We can try to reduce using constraint propagation.\n\nWe can try to solve by constructing possible values for each variable given constraints.\n\nWe have a cycle of constraints linking variables across components:\n\nc1: a,b\nc2: b,c\nc3: c,d\nc4: d,a\n\nThus a,b,c,d form a 4-cycle.\n\nc5-c8: e,f,g,h form a 4-cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e.\n\nc9-c12: i,j,k,l form a 4-cycle: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i.\n\nc13-c16: m,n,o,p form a 4-cycle: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m.\n\nAdditionally cross constraints linking these cycles:\n\nc17: b-e\nc18: f-i\nc19: j-m\nc20: n-a\n\nThus we have a larger graph.\n\nGoal: find all solutions.\n\nWe can try to solve using reasoning.\n\nFirst, constraints c1-c4 linking a,b,c,d.\n\nLet's list all possible assignments for (a,b,c,d) satisfying c1-c4.\n\nc1: allowed (a,b):\n(3,3)\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,2)\n(3,0)\n\nc2: allowed (b,c):\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(2,2)\n(1,3)\n\nc3: allowed (c,d):\n(2,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\nc4: allowed (d,a):\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n\nWe need to find all combos (a,b,c,d) consistent with these.\n\nWe could enumerate possible values for a and b from c1, then possible c from c2 given b, then d from c3 given c, then check c4 for d-a.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nFirst, list possible (a,b) from c1:\n\n1. (a=3, b=3)\n2. (3,2)\n3. (3,1)\n4. (1,3)\n5. (1,2)\n6. (3,0)\n\nNow for each, find possible c from c2 given b.\n\nc2 allowed pairs (b,c):\n- If b=3: allowed c values? The pairs: (3,2) only? Actually c2 includes (3,2) and (1,3). So if b=3, c can be 2 (from (3,2)) or 3 (from (1,3)). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- If b=2: pairs: (3,2) (b=3 not 2). Actually (3,2) has b=3. So not. (0,0) b=0. (0,2) b=0. (1,0) b=1. (2,2) b=2, c=2. (1,3) b=1. So only (2,2) yields b=2. So if b=2, c=2.\n\n- If b=1: pairs: (1,0) (c=0), (1,3) (c=3). So c ∈ {0,3}.\n\n- If b=0: pairs: (0,0) c=0, (0,2) c=2. So c ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow for each (a,b) from c1, we need to see if b is among {3,2,1,0}. All are.\n\nNow for each (a,b) and possible c, we need to find d from c3 (c,d). c3 allowed pairs (c,d):\n(2,3)\n(0,2)\n(2,2)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(1,1)\n\nThus for a given c, possible d values are:\n\n- If c=2: d can be 3 (from (2,3)), 2 (from (2,2)). So d ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- If c=3: d can be 1 (from (1,3)). Actually c=3 appears only in (1,3) where c=1? Wait (1,3) is (c=1,d=3). So c=3 does not appear. So c=3 yields no d? Check c3 list: c values present: 2,0,2,0,1,1. So c values allowed: 0,1,2. No 3. So c cannot be 3. So any (b,c) where c=3 is invalid. So we must discard any combos where c=3.\n\nThus c must be 0,1,2.\n\nNow for each c, possible d:\n\n- c=0: d ∈ {2 (from (0,2)), 1 (from (0,1))}. So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- c=1: d ∈ {3 (from (1,3)), 1 (from (1,1))}. So d ∈ {3,1}.\n\n- c=2: d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nNow we need to check c4: allowed (d,a):\n(3,3)\n(0,2)\n(3,1)\n(3,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n\nThus for each (d,a) pair, we need to ensure that the pair is in this list.\n\nNow we can enumerate all combos.\n\nLet's enumerate each (a,b) from c1, then possible c from c2, then d from c3, then check c4.\n\nWe'll do systematic enumeration.\n\n### Case 1: (a=3, b=3)\n\n- b=3 => c ∈ {2,3} from c2.\nBut c cannot be 3 (invalid). So c must be 2.\n\nThus c=2.\n\nNow c=2 => d ∈ {3,2} from c3.\n\nCheck each d:\n\n- d=3: Check c4 (d=3, a=3). Is (3,3) allowed? Yes, (3,3) is in c4 allowed pairs.\n\n- d=2: Check (d=2, a=3). Is (2,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs: (3,3), (0,2), (3,1), (3,0), (1,1), (3,2). (2,3) is not present. So (2,3) not allowed. So d=2 invalid.\n\nThus only (a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\nSo one solution for (a,b,c,d): (3,3,2,3).\n\n### Case 2: (a=3, b=2)\n\n- b=2 => c must be 2 (from c2). So c=2.\n\nc=2 => d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nCheck d=3: (d=3,a=3) allowed? (3,3) yes.\n\nd=2: (2,3) not allowed. So only d=3 works.\n\nThus (a=3,b=2,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\n### Case 3: (a=3, b=1)\n\n- b=1 => c ∈ {0,3} from c2. c cannot be 3, so c=0.\n\nc=0 => d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCheck d=2: (d=2,a=3) allowed? (2,3) not allowed. So invalid.\n\nd=1: (1,3) allowed? c4 includes (3,1) but that's (d=3,a=1). We need (d=1,a=3). Check c4 list: (3,3), (0,2), (3,1), (3,0), (1,1), (3,2). (1,3) not present. So invalid.\n\nThus no solution for (a=3,b=1).\n\n### Case 4: (a=1, b=3)\n\n- b=3 => c ∈ {2,3}, c cannot be 3, so c=2.\n\nc=2 => d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nCheck d=3: (d=3,a=1) allowed? c4 includes (3,1) which is (d=3,a=1). Yes.\n\nd=2: (2,1) allowed? c4 includes (3,2) but that's (d=3,a=2). (2,1) not present. So only d=3 works.\n\nThus (a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\n### Case 5: (a=1, b=2)\n\n- b=2 => c=2.\n\nc=2 => d ∈ {3,2}.\n\nCheck d=3: (3,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nd=2: (2,1) not allowed. So only d=3.\n\nThus (a=1,b=2,c=2,d=3) valid.\n\n### Case 6: (a=3, b=0)\n\n- b=0 => c ∈ {0,2}.\n\nConsider each:\n\nc=0: then d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- d=2: (d=2,a=3) not allowed.\n\n- d=1: (1,3) not allowed.\n\nThus c=0 yields no solution.\n\nc=2: then d ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- d=3: (3,3) allowed? Yes (a=3). So (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3) works.\n\n- d=2: (2,3) not allowed.\n\nThus (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3) is valid.\n\nThus we have found all (a,b,c,d) solutions:\n\nList them:\n\n1. (a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3)\n2. (a=3,b=2,c=2,d=3)\n3. (a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3)\n4. (a=1,b=2,c=2,d=3)\n5. (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3)\n\nCheck if any other combos? Let's double-check each case for completeness.\n\nWe enumerated all six (a,b) from c1. Cases 1-6 gave solutions for cases 1,2,4,5,6. Cases 3 and maybe others? Let's double-check case 3 (a=3,b=1) gave none. Case 5 (a=1,b=2) gave solution. So total 5 solutions for (a,b,c,d). All have d=3. Interesting.\n\nThus (a,b,c,d) are constrained heavily: a can be 3 or 1, b can be 3,2,0, c always 2, d always 3. Let's verify each solution:\n\n- (3,3,2,3)\n- (3,2,2,3)\n- (1,3,2,3)\n- (1,2,2,3)\n- (3,0,2,3)\n\nThus a ∈ {1,3}, b ∈ {0,2,3} (but not 1), c=2, d=3.\n\nNow we also have cross constraints linking these to other variables:\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (3,1) or (1,1). So (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,1). So e is determined by b:\n\n- If b=3 => e must be 1 (since (3,1) is allowed). (1,1) would require b=1, not applicable.\n\n- If b=1 => e must be 1 (since (1,1) allowed). But b cannot be 1 in our solutions. So b=1 not possible.\n\n- If b=0 => no allowed pair, because allowed pairs for (b,e) are only (3,1) and (1,1). So b cannot be 0. But we have a solution with b=0 (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3). That would violate c17. So that solution is invalid overall. So we must discard any solution where b=0. So (a=3,b=0,c=2,d=3) is invalid due to c17.\n\nThus remaining (a,b,c,d) solutions are those where b ∈ {2,3}. Let's check each:\n\n- (3,3,2,3): b=3 OK.\n- (3,2,2,3): b=2? But c17 only allows b=3 or b=1. b=2 is not allowed. So (3,2,2,3) invalid.\n- (1,3,2,3): b=3 OK.\n- (1,2,2,3): b=2 invalid.\n\nThus only solutions where b=3 survive: (a,b,c,d) = (3,3,2,3) and (1,3,2,3). So two possible assignments for (a,b,c,d) that satisfy c1-c4 and c17.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for (a,b,c,d):\nS1: a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3\nS2: a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3\n\nNow we need to consider constraints linking to other components: c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m), c20 (n,a). Also c5-c8 linking e,f,g,h and c9-c12 linking i,j,k,l and c13-c16 linking m,n,o,p.\n\nAlso there are constraints linking these cycles via cross constraints: c17 (b,e) already used to restrict e based on b; c18 (f,i); c19 (j,m); c20 (n,a).\n\nThus we need to find all assignments for the rest of variables consistent with these constraints and the cycles.\n\nWe also have constraints linking within each cycle: c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) for e,f,g,h. So they form a 4-cycle.\n\nSimilarly i,j,k,l cycle: c9 (i,j), c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i).\n\nSimilarly m,n,o,p cycle: c13 (m,n), c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m).\n\nThus we have three independent cycles plus cross constraints linking b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a.\n\nGiven we have two possible (a,b) values (a=3,b=3) or (a=1,b=3). In both cases b=3. So b is fixed to 3. So we have b=3 for both solutions.\n\nThus c17 (b,e) => (3,e) must be allowed. Allowed pairs: (3,1) only (since (1,1) not applicable). So e must be 1. So e=1 for both solutions.\n\nThus e is fixed: e=1.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (1,3), (2,1), (0,3), (1,0), (2,2), (0,1). Since e=1, we need (1,f) in allowed list. Let's check allowed pairs for (e,f) where e=1:\n\nFrom c5 allowed pairs:\n- (1,3) => f=3\n- (1,0) => f=0\n- (1,?) any others? (2,1) e=2, not. (0,3) e=0. (2,2) e=2. (0,1) e=0.\n\nThus only (1,3) and (1,0) are possible for e=1. So f ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus f can be 3 or 0.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (3,1), (2,1), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3), (2,3). So depending on f.\n\n- If f=3: allowed g values from c6: (3,1) => g=1. Also (3,?) any others? No other pair with f=3. So g=1.\n\n- If f=0: allowed g values: (0,1) => g=1; (0,3) => g=3; (0,?) also (0,?) maybe (0,?) only those two. So g ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus possible combos:\n\nCase A: f=3 => g=1.\n\nCase B: f=0 => g ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (1,1), (1,0), (0,2), (3,1), (0,1), (3,3). So given g, possible h values:\n\n- If g=1: allowed h: (1,1) => h=1; (1,0) => h=0. Also (3,1) is g=3 not relevant. So h ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h: (3,1) => h=1; (3,3) => h=3. So h ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus for each case:\n\nCase A: f=3,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0}.\n\nCase B1: f=0,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0}.\n\nCase B2: f=0,g=3 => h ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (1,1), (3,1), (0,3), (2,2), (3,2), (2,0). Since e=1, we need (h,1) allowed. So we need pairs where second component is 1: (1,1) => h=1; (3,1) => h=3. Also (0,3) second is 3 not relevant; (2,2) second is 2; (3,2) second 2; (2,0) second 0. So only allowed h values with e=1 are h=1 (via (1,1)) and h=3 (via (3,1)). So h must be either 1 or 3.\n\nThus from earlier possibilities, we need to intersect with allowed h values {1,3}.\n\nLet's examine each case:\n\nCase A: f=3,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0} from c7. Intersection with {1,3} yields h=1 only. So h=1.\n\nThus case A yields (f=3,g=1,h=1). Check c8: (h=1,e=1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is allowed.\n\nCase B1: f=0,g=1 => h ∈ {1,0}. Intersection with {1,3} yields h=1 only. So h=1.\n\nThus (f=0,g=1,h=1). Check c8: (1,1) allowed.\n\nCase B2: f=0,g=3 => h ∈ {1,3}. Intersection with {1,3} yields h ∈ {1,3}. So both h=1 and h=3 possible.\n\nThus we have three possible subcases:\n\n- Subcase 1: f=3,g=1,h=1.\n- Subcase 2: f=0,g=1,h=1.\n- Subcase 3a: f=0,g=3,h=1.\n- Subcase 3b: f=0,g=3,h=3.\n\nNow we also have cross constraint c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (3,0) and (1,3). So (f,i) must be either (3,0) or (1,3). So we need to consider f values and possible i.\n\nGiven f possibilities: f=3 or f=0.\n\n- If f=3: then (f,i) must be (3,0). So i=0.\n\n- If f=0: then (f,i) must be (0,?) but allowed pairs are (3,0) and (1,3). No (0,?) pair. So f=0 is not allowed because there is no allowed pair with f=0. Wait check c18: allowed pairs: [3,0], [1,3]. So (f,i) can be (3,0) or (1,3). So f can be 3 (with i=0) or f can be 1 (with i=3). But we have f ∈ {3,0} from earlier. So f=0 is not allowed because there is no allowed pair with f=0. So f cannot be 0. So the only viable f is 3. So f must be 3, i=0.\n\nThus we discard all subcases where f=0. So only Subcase 1 remains: f=3,g=1,h=1,i=0.\n\nThus e=1, f=3, g=1, h=1, i=0.\n\nNow we have determined e,f,g,h uniquely: (e=1,f=3,g=1,h=1). So the tuple for c2 is (e,f,g,h) = (1,3,1,1). That's one tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify all constraints are satisfied. Let's verify all constraints involving these variables:\n\nc5 (e,f): (1,3) is allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c5 allowed pairs.\n\nc6 (f,g): (3,1) is allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c6.\n\nc7 (g,h): (1,1) is allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c7.\n\nc8 (h,e): (1,1) is allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c8.\n\nc17 (b,e): b=3, e=1 => (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is allowed.\n\nc18 (f,i): f=3,i=0 => (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus all constraints for c2 and cross constraints are satisfied.\n\nNow we need to consider the rest of the variables: i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p and constraints c9-c16, plus cross constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). Also constraints linking these cycles.\n\nWe have determined i=0. Now we need to find all possible assignments for the rest that satisfy constraints.\n\nNow we need to consider the cycle i-j-k-l (c9-c12). Variables: i=0, j, k, l.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (2,3), (1,1), (3,2), (1,0). Since i=0, we need (0,j) allowed. Allowed pairs with i=0: (0,2) => j=2; (0,3) => j=3. Also (0,?) any others? (0,?) appears only in (0,2) and (0,3). So j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,3), (1,0), (1,1), (0,0), (0,3). So depending on j.\n\n- If j=2: allowed k: (2,2) => k=2. Also any other pairs with j=2? No other. So k=2.\n\n- If j=3: allowed k: (0,3) => k=3? Wait (0,3) is (j=0,k=3). Not j=3. (1,3) is (j=1,k=3). Not j=3. So there is no pair with j=3? Let's check c10 allowed pairs: [2,2], [1,3], [1,0], [1,1], [0,0], [0,3]. So j values present: 2,1,1,1,0,0. So j=3 does not appear. So j cannot be 3. Thus j must be 2.\n\nThus j=2, k=2.\n\nNow c11 (k,l): allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (1,2), (0,1), (3,2). Since k=2, we need (2,l) allowed. Check pairs with first component 2: (2,0) => l=0. Also (2,?) any others? No other. So l=0.\n\nThus l=0.\n\nNow c12 (l,i): allowed pairs: (0,0), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1), (3,2), (2,3). Since l=0, i=0, we need (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in c12 allowed pairs. So (l=0,i=0) is allowed.\n\nThus the i-j-k-l cycle is uniquely determined: (i=0,j=2,k=2,l=0).\n\nNow we have cross constraints: c19 (j,m): allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). Since j=2, we need (2,m) allowed. Allowed pairs: (2,0) => m=0. Also (3,1) not applicable. So m=0.\n\nThus m=0.\n\nNow we have c13 (m,n): allowed pairs: (0,3), (2,2), (0,2), (1,3), (2,3), (3,1). Since m=0, we need (0,n) allowed. Allowed pairs with first component 0: (0,3) => n=3; (0,2) => n=2. So n ∈ {3,2}.\n\nThus n ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c14 (n,o): allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (0,0), (1,3), (2,1), (1,1). So depending on n.\n\n- If n=2: allowed (2,3) => o=3; (2,1) => o=1. Also (0,0) not; (1,3) not; (3,2) not; (1,1) not. So o ∈ {3,1}.\n\n- If n=3: allowed (3,2) => o=2; (1,3) => o=3; (2,3) => o=3? Actually (2,3) is (n=2) not relevant. (1,1) not. So for n=3: (3,2) => o=2; (1,3) => o=3. Also maybe (2,3) is not n=3. So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus possible combos:\n\nn=2 => o ∈ {3,1}\nn=3 => o ∈ {2,3}\n\nNow c15 (o,p): allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1), (2,2), (1,2), (1,0), (3,3). So depending on o.\n\n- If o=3: allowed p: (3,1) => p=1; (3,3) => p=3. Also (1,2) not; (2,2) not; (2,0) not; (1,0) not.\n\nThus o=3 => p ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- If o=2: allowed p: (2,0) => p=0; (2,2) => p=2; (1,2) => p=2? Actually (1,2) is (o=2? No (1,2) is (o=2? Wait c15 is (o,p). So (1,2) means o=1,p=2. So not relevant. So for o=2: p ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- If o=1: allowed p: (1,2) => p=2; (1,0) => p=0. So p ∈ {2,0}.\n\nThus we need to consider each possible n and o, then p.\n\nNow c16 (p,m): allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3), (1,0), (3,0). Since m=0, we need (p,0) allowed. So allowed pairs where second component is 0: (0,0) => p=0; (1,0) => p=1; (3,0) => p=3. Also (2,3) second is 3; (3,2) second 2; (1,1) second 1. So p ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nThus p must be in {0,1,3}.\n\nNow we need to find assignments for n,o,p that satisfy c14, c15, c16 constraints, plus p ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nLet's enumerate possibilities.\n\nWe have two possible n values: 2 or 3.\n\n### n=2:\n\n- o ∈ {3,1}.\n\n#### n=2, o=3:\n\n- c15: o=3 => p ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {1,3} = {1,3}. So p ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus possible (n=2,o=3,p=1) or (n=2,o=3,p=3).\n\nCheck c16 for p=1: (p=1,m=0) => (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is allowed. For p=3: (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is allowed.\n\nThus both are valid.\n\n#### n=2, o=1:\n\n- o=1 => p ∈ {2,0} from c15.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {2,0} = {0}. So p must be 0.\n\nThus (n=2,o=1,p=0) is valid. Check c15: (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is allowed. c16: (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) allowed.\n\nThus (n=2,o=1,p=0) valid.\n\nThus for n=2 we have three possible assignments:\n\n1. (n=2, o=3, p=1)\n2. (n=2, o=3, p=3)\n3. (n=2, o=1, p=0)\n\n### n=3:\n\n- o ∈ {2,3}.\n\n#### n=3, o=2:\n\n- o=2 => p ∈ {0,2} from c15.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {0,2} = {0}. So p=0.\n\nThus (n=3, o=2, p=0) valid. Check c15: (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) allowed. c16: (0,0) allowed? Yes.\n\n#### n=3, o=3:\n\n- o=3 => p ∈ {1,3} from c15.\n\n- c16: p ∈ {0,1,3} ∩ {1,3} = {1,3}. So p ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus (n=3, o=3, p=1) and (n=3, o=3, p=3) are valid.\n\nCheck c15: (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) allowed. (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) allowed. c16: (1,0) allowed? Yes. (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus for n=3 we have three possible assignments:\n\n1. (n=3, o=2, p=0)\n2. (n=3, o=3, p=1)\n3. (n=3, o=3, p=3)\n\nThus total possible assignments for (n,o,p) are 6.\n\nNow we also have cross constraint c20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (3,3) and (3,0). So (n,a) must be either (3,3) or (3,0). So n must be 3 (since both allowed pairs have n=3). So n must be 3. Therefore n=2 possibilities are invalid because n must be 3. So we discard all assignments where n=2.\n\nThus only assignments with n=3 remain.\n\nThus we have three possible assignments for (n,o,p) given n=3:\n\n- (n=3, o=2, p=0)\n- (n=3, o=3, p=1)\n- (n=3, o=3, p=3)\n\nNow we also need to consider any other constraints linking these variables. c13 (m,n) already satisfied with m=0,n=3? Let's check c13: allowed pairs (0,3) and (0,2) etc. (0,3) is allowed. So (m=0,n=3) is allowed.\n\nc14 (n,o) we already used.\n\nc15 (o,p) we used.\n\nc16 (p,m) we used.\n\nThus all constraints for m,n,o,p are satisfied for these three assignments.\n\nNow we also need to consider any constraints linking these to other components: cross constraints c19 (j,m) already satisfied (j=2,m=0). c20 (n,a) must be satisfied. Since a is either 3 or 1 (from earlier solutions). Let's check each possible a.\n\nWe have two possible (a,b,c,d) solutions: S1: a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3; S2: a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3.\n\nNow c20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (3,3) and (3,0). So n must be 3, a must be 3 (since (3,0) would require a=0). So a must be 3. So a=3 is required. Therefore the solution with a=1 is invalid. So we must discard S2 (a=1,b=3,c=2,d=3). So only S1 remains: a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3.\n\nThus we have a=3,b=3,c=2,d=3.\n\nNow we need to ensure all constraints are satisfied with this assignment.\n\nWe already checked c1-c4, c17, c5-c8, c9-c12, c13-c16, c18, c19, c20. Let's verify each for the final assignments.\n\nWe have determined:\n\n- a=3\n- b=3\n- c=2\n- d=3\n- e=1\n- f=3\n- g=1\n- h=1\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=2\n- l=0\n- m=0\n- n=3 (one of the three possibilities)\n- o and p depend on case.\n\nNow we need to verify all constraints for each of the three possible (n,o,p) assignments.\n\nConstraints list:\n\nc1 (a,b): (3,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc2 (b,c): (3,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nc3 (c,d): (2,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc4 (d,a): (3,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc5 (e,f): (1,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc6 (f,g): (3,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc7 (g,h): (1,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc8 (h,e): (1,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc9 (i,j): (0,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nc10 (j,k): (2,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nc11 (k,l): (2,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc12 (l,i): (0,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc13 (m,n): (0,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nc14 (n,o): depends on o.\n\n- For (n=3,o=2): (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c14 allowed pairs.\n\n- For (n=3,o=3): (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is allowed.\n\nc15 (o,p): depends.\n\n- For (o=2,p=0): (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c15.\n\n- For (o=3,p=1): (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c15.\n\n- For (o=3,p=3): (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in c15.\n\nc16 (p,m): (p,0) must be allowed.\n\n- For p=0: (0,0) allowed? Yes.\n\n- For p=1: (1,0) allowed? Yes.\n\n- For p=3: (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc17 (b,e): (3,1) allowed? Yes.\n\nc18 (f,i): (3,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc19 (j,m): (2,0) allowed? Yes.\n\nc20 (n,a): (3,3) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied for all three possibilities of (n,o,p). So we have three distinct global solutions differing only in (n,o,p). However, the question asks to \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c2, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to collect all possible assignments for component c2 across all global solutions. Since c2 variables are e,f,g,h, which we have uniquely determined as (1,3,1,1) across all solutions (the three solutions share same e,f,g,h). So there is only one tuple for c2.\n\nThus the answer should be a JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing a single tuple: [1,3,1,1] (in order e,f,g,h). The envelope template currently has placeholder [[0,0,0,0]]. We need to replace that with the correct tuple(s). The envelope also includes other fields that we must keep unchanged except for the tuples. The envelope template includes fields like \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", etc. We need to output exactly that JSON with the updated tuples.\n\nThus final envelope:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6432-c2-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c2-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6432-c2-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c2\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            3,\n            1,\n            1\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6432\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c1-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure JSON formatting exactly matches. No extra spaces? The template has spaces. We can preserve similar formatting. The requirement: \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output raw JSON as above, with the tuple replaced. Ensure we keep the same ordering of keys as in template. The template includes \"payload\": { \"claim\": {...} }. The \"value\" includes \"component\": \"c2\", \"tuples\": [ [1,3,1,1] ], \"variables\": [ \"e\", \"f\", \"g\", \"h\" ].\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope with that tuple.\n\nWe should double-check that there are no other possible assignments for c2 that could arise from other global solutions that we might have missed. Let's verify if there could be any alternative assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints but we elim